|title=w:ru:Мелкий бес|chapter=XXI|location=|publisher=|newversion=English translation from|translator2=John Cournos and Richard Aldington|title2=w:[[The Petty Demon|The Little Demon]]|location2=New York|publisher2=w:en:Alfred A. Knopf|year2=1916|page2=|pageurl2=
|text=В воскресе́нье, '''когда́''' Передо́нов и Варва́ра за́втракали, в пере́днюю кто́-то вошёл.|t=On Sunday '''when''' Peredonov and Varvara were lunching, someone entered the hall.}}
#* {{quote-book|ru|year=1979|author=w:ru:Эдуард Лимонов<t:{{w|Eduard Limonov}}>|title=w:ru:Это я — Эдичка|chapter=Глава первая. Отель «Винслоу» и его обитатели|location=|publisher=Альпина Диджитал|newversion=English translation from|translator2=S. L. Campbell|title2=w:It's Me, Eddie|location2=|publisher2=|year2=1983|page2=|pageurl2=|text=Он звони́т лю́дям, кото́рых он оди́н раз в жи́зни ви́дел, и про́сит де́нег, и о́чень обижа́ется, '''когда́''' ему́ отка́зывают.|t=He calls up people he has seen once in his life and asks for money, and is very offended '''when''' they refuse him.}}

====Usage notes====
* Both the subordinate clause with the conjunction '''когда''' and the main clause can have either an imperfective or a perfective verb. However, the whole context of the respective sentences could be different due to the difference of the aspects.
* Chiefly in colloquial language, the conjunction ''когда'' can be moved to any position in the dependent clause including the final one; this typically occurs in short introductory clauses that recall or specify being in the middle of things of a situation.

